00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:55.720
But it comes with its
own set of challenges.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.850
So it is slow, it's
often stored off site,

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:01.790
and I'm preaching to
the choir here for sure.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:04.462
But it is slow, it
is stored off site,

00:12:04.462 --> 00:12:06.295
it can take several
hours for you to restore

00:12:06.295 --> 00:12:08.830
or several days
for you to restore.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:10.420
And then it's manual.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:14.650
We had a customer which had
10 plus petabytes of data that

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:17.470
was basically backups that
they had done over the years

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:20.680
and they wanted to know how
they can move that to the cloud.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:23.740
The big challenge was
based on the resourcing

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:25.330
they had done the
math and it would

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:27.160
have taken several
months for somebody

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:29.980
to just read those tapes.

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.980
So it becomes a challenge,
it's a manual steps.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:36.970
And then whenever you
have lots of manual steps,

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:40.780
Murphy's law comes into effect
and it's always error prone.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:46.600
And then disk is less manual,
it's relatively easier

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:49.250
to manage, but it is expensive.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:52.090
It's not meant for long
term durable storage

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:55.480
and it doesn't scale that well.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:59.020
So that is why we believe
in backup to the cloud

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.510
and we obviously have
a vested interest

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:05.510
but we believe that cloud
comes with its advantages.

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:10.030
For example, reliability as
Ben said with cloud storage

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:13.010
